x\\in [2,6].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is undefined."} :step/id #uuid "2ec792e1-dee9-5e77-b785-5597e0c43364" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573343}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ddabc6-6948-5d05-984f-8724d78f9060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572845} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac77e93-6655-5cb9-83e2-8d0071ffa65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572853} {:answer/id #uuid "23691b4e-ef96-59e7-b343-43a0604ca109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572868} {:answer/id #uuid "7059fd79-bfc5-56de-bb09-06241fc81d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572964} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcb3571-4895-5ea8-a376-01f2897be6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572868} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c3ab6b7-6e70-535c-9a1b-8fc6113a3975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "002cab17-2b93-5a94-bc24-04ae7b92f4f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152871" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi :$   \n\n![](images/42/42eb38a11d9d63e05c0078bae4c052566b3162571e8563f22cd52ae31bc6dbd7.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=4$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4ba79a17-62dc-5f7c-94c1-fc0884ba6fdc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d9ec78-8e28-521d-942f-9298b56ba664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272678} {:answer/id #uuid "37ff1036-5a0e-58b1-9825-df07520b3d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272877} {:answer/id #uuid "8cabb11a-cc05-5405-8952-4408c4076c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273602} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ac53fc-a437-5933-a0ee-8ea304f15e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273915} {:answer/id #uuid "f117b36f-681e-570c-bee9-72f13571afe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272678} :answer-field/id #uuid "a79374a6-9789-530a-8830-fd0dab1ccf3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "81deaa75-bb15-5048-ab19-c54290c068b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-149955" :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1):$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2e705cf17a5dca362c23a23e4d10067ff4390a3f9464dddb6745d78986c02a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is $x\\in [0,1].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined."} :step/id #uuid "a0212770-e29d-50bd-b4a4-f099eaf2835b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cab12af0-b59f-5bad-b4db-8eea41c70809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573080} :answer-field/id #uuid "52153792-c358-5ad7-a1f9-bbbc25297261" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f397079-49a9-58d1-bc93-fa23602eefa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344566" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/86/86c0cf22de78e3b773543680c2ace077f97b66ae822436d637c5444903e2330f.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)) & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a502c7a6-6f04-5265-9f8d-a0ce92311190"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa045d5-9683-533e-b3b9-383180919e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315174} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c61dd80-a3fe-57a0-9d9a-a6d5e5d6f81c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b99f3255-7a91-5700-9ad5-6321b83c8397" :question/math-academy-id "q-343225" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff0c03ef-8677-5c60-a9b8-6870eff0abc1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573357}}] :activity/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572822}]}
